.","rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 44\u00b0F was the 27th coolest November 13 high (tied) of 138 since 1889, the coolest since 2022.","t_yest":"November 13: cooler than 79% of years","p_yest":"The high of <strong>44\u00b0F</strong> was the 27th coolest November 13 high (tied) of 138 since 1889, the coolest since 2022. The normal high for the date is 55\u00b0F, and the record is 82\u00b0F (1999). The low of <strong>29\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Columbia has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Columbia's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Columbia has set <strong class=\"sig\">23 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 72 that chance alone would give, and <strong>67 record highs</strong>, close to the 72 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (59). Columbia's all-time high is 113\u00b0F, on July 12, 1954.","t_chg":"Columbia's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 135 complete years: Columbia's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.12\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.05 to +0.19), about +1.6\u00b0F since 1890. Columbia's average high shows no clear change: +0.04\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.04 to +0.11) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.83\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.44 to +1.23) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.65\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +1.13). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in 2 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Columbia's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.27 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 41.4 inches a year. The wettest year was 1993 (62.49 in); the driest, 1901 (21.35 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 6 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}